    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let's review the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хорошо с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I feel good with you.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'good' with 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Мне тоже хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I feel good too.' Sasha links 'to me' directly with 'too' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Особенно когда ты без одежды.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Especially when you are without clothes.' Notice how 'especially' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'clothes'. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты очень смешная.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are very funny.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'you' directly with 'funny'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Я серьезно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am serious.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'seriously'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I know.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'know'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Тебе вызвать такси?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Should I call you a taxi?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'for you' with 'taxi'. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Зачем?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why?' Max asks this with a single word meaning 'what for', using rising intonation to turn it into a quick question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Ты сегодня без машины.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are without a car today.' Sasha uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'cars', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Как ты будешь добираться домой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How will you get home?' Notice how 'how' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'home'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я поеду на метро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will go by subway.' Max combines 'I' with 'metro' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Sasha responds.",
